Memories in both ChatGPT and Codex are a game-changer. They allow these models to contextualize and hyper-specialize their outputs based on your preferences.

The user is often amazed by how Codex can recall tiny details, such as which tests to run, commit dynamics, and design choices, all learned from past sessions. They highly recommend enabling this feature.

@nivi: "Memory in ChatGPT has gotten insanely good and nobody is talking about it. It shocks me daily."

A couple of days ago, @swyx posted a challenge: code a ~10M transformer in JAX/Flax/Optax, run it in free Colab, and train it on addition with your agent. I gave Codex the screenshot and the goal.

It controlled Colab through Chrome, used my signed-in session, handled runtime and editing issues, ran the T4 job, and then used subagents to audit the result. The end state was 10,652,557 parameters, ~19 minutes of training, and 99 out of 100 exact random checks. Still needs cleaner evaluations, but autonomously babysitting the training run over Chrome is pretty wild.

@reach_vb: "Couple days back @swyx posted a challenge: code a roughly 10 million parameter transformer in JAX, Flax, and Optax, run it in free Colab, and train it on simple addition."

